    {
      "id": "place-line",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 46,
      "purpose": "ALREADY CHOSEN, reopenable.  The shape of the one line that says where the data lives:  your own name, a kind, an address.",
      "note": "Spanish:  `recetario familiar es un servicio web en \"https://api.example.com/v1\"`\nToday:  Runs:  the recipe box, on real servers, in a browser.\nA (built, recommended):  `family cookbook is a web service at \"https://api.example.com/v1\"`  --  chosen by you (Q2).  Same shape as `task list is an app`, and a library can add a kind.\nB:  `family cookbook is at \"https://api.example.com/v1\"`  ·  `recetario familiar está en \"https://api.example.com/v1\"`  --  the shortest, but Spell would have to guess the kind, and REST and GraphQL both use https.\nC:  `family cookbook is a place at \"https://api.example.com/v1\"`  ·  `recetario familiar es un lugar en \"https://api.example.com/v1\"`  --  already compiles in English, with no kind to go on, and is refused in Spanish.  The same guessing.\nWhy:  Decided, and it held up on the real parser.  Not offered:  `source` (Spell UI's sources, source code), `store` (Spell's store classes;  `tienda` is a shop).\nRenaming it later:  20 lines in 16 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "family cookbook is a web service at \"https://api.example.com/v1\"",
      "recommended": "family cookbook is a web service at \"https://api.example.com/v1\""
    },
    {
      "id": "web-service",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 39,
      "purpose": "A server you reach over the web, by its usual addresses (REST, to a programmer).",
      "note": "Spanish:  `recetario familiar es un servicio web en \"https://api.example.com/v1\"`\nToday:  Runs:  the recipe box, on a real REST server.\nA (built, recommended):  `family cookbook is a web service at \"https://api.example.com/v1\"`  --  what the people who run one call it, and Spanish says it the same.\nB:  `family cookbook is a web server at \"https://api.example.com/v1\"`  ·  `recetario familiar es un servidor web en \"https://api.example.com/v1\"`  --  the machine, not what it offers;  also what serves plain pages.\nC:  `family cookbook is a website at \"https://api.example.com/v1\"`  ·  `recetario familiar es un sitio web en \"https://api.example.com/v1\"`  --  lay people know the word, but a website shows pages:  it reads as scraping one.\nWhy:  Neither alternative says something a program can ask.\nRenaming it later:  47 lines in 25 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "family cookbook is a web service at \"https://api.example.com/v1\"",
      "recommended": "family cookbook is a web service at \"https://api.example.com/v1\""
    },
    {
      "id": "graph-service",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 39,
      "purpose": "A server that answers GraphQL:  the program says exactly which fields it wants.",
      "note": "Spanish:  `recetario familiar es un servicio de grafos en \"https://api.example.com/gql\"`\nToday:  Runs:  the recipe box, on a real GraphQL server.\nA (built):  `family cookbook is a graph service at \"https://api.example.com/gql\"`  --  parallel with `web service`, but nobody calls theirs that, and `grafos` reads as graph theory.\nB (recommended):  `family cookbook is a graphql service at \"https://api.example.com/gql\"`  ·  `recetario familiar es un servicio graphql en \"https://api.example.com/gql\"`  --  the word on the server's own docs, so someone handed an address knows which line to write.  Product names are already fine here (`airtable base`).\nC:  `family cookbook is a query service at \"https://api.example.com/gql\"`  ·  `recetario familiar es un servicio de consultas en \"https://api.example.com/gql\"`  --  plain, but says nothing that tells it from a web service.\nWhy:  The one place line where the plain word hides what the reader needs to match.  This is a change from what's built.\nRenaming it later:  15 lines in 8 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "family cookbook is a graph service at \"https://api.example.com/gql\"",
      "recommended": "family cookbook is a graphql service at \"https://api.example.com/gql\""
    },
    {
      "id": "folder",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 39,
      "purpose": "A folder of files on a server, one small file per chunk of data, kept in step for you.",
      "note": "Spanish:  `recetario familiar es una carpeta en \"recetas\"`\nToday:  Runs:  the recipe box, on a real folder behind the place server.\nA (built, recommended):  `family cookbook is a folder at \"recipes\"`  --  everyone's word.\nB:  `family cookbook is a directory at \"recipes\"`  ·  `recetario familiar es un directorio en \"recetas\"`  --  a programmer's word for the same thing.\nC:  `family cookbook is a shared folder at \"recipes\"`  ·  `recetario familiar es una carpeta compartida en \"recetas\"`  --  true when others use it, but says more than the kind does, and suggests a Dropbox one.\nWhy:  Nothing to gain.  A person's own `file` and `sheet` properties keep their meaning:  the kinds are a word rule, not types every program would lose.\nRenaming it later:  2 lines in 2 files of rules, tests and docs, 1 line in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "family cookbook is a folder at \"recipes\"",
      "recommended": "family cookbook is a folder at \"recipes\""
    },
    {
      "id": "file",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 39,
      "purpose": "One file holding all of the data.",
      "note": "Spanish:  `recetario familiar es un archivo en \"recetas.json5\"`\nToday:  Compiles, in both languages;  the engine's file place is tested, but the recipe box isn't run on one.\nA (built, recommended):  `family cookbook is a file at \"recipes.json5\"`  --  everyone's word.\nB:  `family cookbook is a document at \"recipes.json5\"`  ·  `recetario familiar es un documento en \"recetas.json5\"`  --  reads as a Word file or a page.\nC:  `family cookbook is a json file at \"recipes.json5\"`  ·  `recetario familiar es un archivo json en \"recetas.json5\"`  --  precise, but the address says that already, and it adds a term.\nWhy:  Nothing to gain.\nRenaming it later:  5 lines in 3 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "family cookbook is a file at \"recipes.json5\"",
      "recommended": "family cookbook is a file at \"recipes.json5\""
    },
    {
      "id": "sheet",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.es.ts",
      "line": 45,
      "purpose": "A spreadsheet's rows, read only:  a write won't compile, and says why.",
      "note": "Spanish:  `recetario familiar es una hoja en \"recetas.csv\"`\nToday:  Runs:  the recipe box, on a real CSV;  its Spanish is compiled, not run on a sheet.\nA (built):  `family cookbook is a sheet at \"recipes.csv\"`  --  short, and `Sheets` is a word people know.  But `hoja` alone is a sheet of paper, or a leaf.\nB (recommended):  es:  recetario familiar es una hoja de cálculo en \"recetas.csv\"  --  English stays;  Spanish says what everyone there calls a spreadsheet.\nC:  `family cookbook is a spreadsheet at \"recipes.csv\"`  ·  `recetario familiar es una hoja de cálculo en \"recetas.csv\"`  --  the everyday word in both;  longer in English, for no extra meaning.\nWhy:  A change to the Spanish only, from what's built.  Multi-word kinds already read (`sala en vivo`).\nRenaming it later:  146 lines in 47 files of rules, tests and docs, 115 lines in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "family cookbook is a sheet at \"recipes.csv\"",
      "recommended": "es:  recetario familiar es una hoja de cálculo en \"recetas.csv\""
    },
    {
      "id": "live-room",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 39,
      "purpose": "A server that people's pages stay connected to, so each sees the others' changes at once.",
      "note": "Spanish:  `recetario familiar es una sala en vivo en \"wss://rooms.example.com/family\"`\nToday:  Runs:  the recipe box, on a real room.\nA (built, recommended):  `family cookbook is a live room at \"wss://rooms.example.com/family\"`  --  says what it feels like:  everyone in one room, live.  Multiplayer tools say `room` too.\nB:  `family cookbook is a shared room at \"wss://rooms.example.com/family\"`  ·  `recetario familiar es una sala compartida en \"wss://rooms.example.com/family\"`  --  `shared` says less:  a folder is shared too.\nC:  `family cookbook is a room at \"wss://rooms.example.com/family\"`  ·  `recetario familiar es una sala en \"wss://rooms.example.com/family\"`  --  shorter, but a program's own `room` is a likely name.\nWhy:  Not offered:  `live space`, since spaces are where Spell keeps projects.\nRenaming it later:  21 lines in 13 files of rules, tests and docs, 2 lines in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "family cookbook is a live room at \"wss://rooms.example.com/family\"",
      "recommended": "family cookbook is a live room at \"wss://rooms.example.com/family\""
    },
    {
      "id": "airtable",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/tests/spell/parserTests/wordsPrototype.test.ts",
      "line": 58,
      "purpose": "A base in the Airtable product, behind a small server of ours, since its key can't reach a browser.",
      "note": "Spanish:  `recetario familiar es una base de airtable en \"appXXXXXXXXXXXXXX\"`\nToday:  Prototype only:  reads on the test parser;  the real parser refuses it until the Airtable phase.\nA (built, recommended):  `family cookbook is an airtable base at \"appXXXXXXXXXXXXXX\"`  --  Airtable's own word for it.\nB:  `family cookbook is an airtable at \"appXXXXXXXXXXXXXX\"`  ·  `recetario familiar es un airtable en \"appXXXXXXXXXXXXXX\"`  --  how people say it aloud, but no such thing is called that.\nC:  `family cookbook is an airtable table at \"tblXXXXXXXXXXXXXX\"`  ·  `recetario familiar es una tabla de airtable en \"tblXXXXXXXXXXXXXX\"`  --  one table:  but a program may save several types in one place.\nWhy:  Matches what a person sees in Airtable.\nRenaming it later:  0 lines in 0 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "family cookbook is an airtable base at \"appXXXXXXXXXXXXXX\"",
      "recommended": "family cookbook is an airtable base at \"appXXXXXXXXXXXXXX\""
    },
    {
      "id": "atproto",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/tests/spell/parserTests/wordsPrototype.test.ts",
      "line": 59,
      "purpose": "One person's account on the network Bluesky runs on, holding their records.",
      "note": "Spanish:  `recetario familiar es una cuenta de at protocol en \"did:plc:abc123\"`\nToday:  Prototype only:  reads on the test parser;  the real parser refuses it until the AT Protocol phase.\nA (built, recommended):  `family cookbook is an at protocol account at \"did:plc:abc123\"`  --  exact, works for any host.  `at` twice in a row reads oddly, but it does parse.\nB:  `family cookbook is a bluesky account at \"did:plc:abc123\"`  ·  `recetario familiar es una cuenta de bluesky en \"did:plc:abc123\"`  --  the name people know, but untrue of an account on another host.\nC:  `family cookbook is an atproto account at \"did:plc:abc123\"`  ·  `recetario familiar es una cuenta de atproto en \"did:plc:abc123\"`  --  fixes the doubled `at`, with a word only developers use.\nWhy:  Not built yet, so the cheapest row on the page to change later.\nRenaming it later:  0 lines in 0 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "family cookbook is an at protocol account at \"did:plc:abc123\"",
      "recommended": "family cookbook is an at protocol account at \"did:plc:abc123\""
    },
    {
      "id": "atproto-each",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/tests/spell/parserTests/wordsPrototype.test.ts",
      "line": 206,
      "purpose": "No one address:  each person's records live in their own account, and a read puts everyone's together.",
      "note": "Spanish:  `recetario familiar es la propia cuenta de at protocol de cada persona:`\nToday:  Prototype only:  the one place line that doesn't fit `is a ... at`, so it has a rule of its own.\nA (built, recommended):  `family cookbook is each person's own at protocol account:`  --  says who holds what, with no address to invent.\nB:  `family cookbook is an at protocol account for each person:`  ·  `recetario familiar es una cuenta de at protocol por persona:`  --  keeps `is an ...`, which is closer to the other kinds.\nC:  `each person keeps a family cookbook in their at protocol account:`  ·  `cada persona guarda un recetario familiar en su cuenta de at protocol:`  --  the plainest sentence, but it no longer starts with the place's name, which every other line does.\nWhy:  Keep until the phase is built, then look at it again in a real program.\nRenaming it later:  0 lines in 0 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "family cookbook is each person's own at protocol account:",
      "recommended": "family cookbook is each person's own at protocol account:"
    },
    {
      "id": "this-device",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 43,
      "purpose": "Kept on the machine in front of you, with no server at all.",
      "note": "Spanish:  `recetario familiar es este dispositivo`\nToday:  Runs:  it's what the built-in Recipe Box uses, in English and Spanish.\nA (built, recommended):  `family cookbook is this device`  --  what phones and apps say (`save to this device`), and true on any screen.\nB:  `family cookbook is this browser`  ·  `recetario familiar es este navegador`  --  more exact:  another browser on the same machine won't see it, and the example's own comment says browser.  A word for a program that may not stay in one.\nC:  `family cookbook is this computer`  ·  `recetario familiar es este ordenador`  --  untrue on a phone, and `computadora` / `ordenador` split Spanish.\nWhy:  Keep, though it's a close call with `this browser`.\nRenaming it later:  19 lines in 13 files of rules, tests and docs, 1 line in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "family cookbook is this device",
      "recommended": "family cookbook is this device"
    },
    {
      "id": "in-memory",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 43,
      "purpose": "Kept only while the page is open;  gone on reload.  Mostly for tests and trying a program out.",
      "note": "Spanish:  `recetario familiar está en memoria`\nToday:  Runs:  the parser's own tests use it.\nA (built, recommended):  `family cookbook is in memory`  --  exact, and `en memoria` is fine, but it's a programmer's phrase.\nB:  `family cookbook is temporary`  ·  `recetario familiar es temporal`  --  plain, and says the thing that matters.  But it's a kind of word, not a place, and the survey didn't weigh it.\nC:  `family cookbook is nowhere`  ·  `recetario familiar no está en ningún lugar`  --  funny and honest, and useless in Spanish.\nWhy:  A type with no place is already kept in memory, so few people will ever write this.\nRenaming it later:  38 lines in 25 files of rules, tests and docs, 1 line in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "family cookbook is in memory",
      "recommended": "family cookbook is in memory"
    },
    {
      "id": "cloud",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 43,
      "purpose": "A place Spell would host for you, so a first program needs no setup.",
      "note": "Spanish:  `recetario familiar es la nube`\nToday:  Refused for now:  it reads, and says the cloud isn't here yet;  that phase is after the other back-ends.\nA (built, recommended):  `family cookbook is the cloud`  --  your own sentence.  A survey found people's picture of it is fuzzy and they use it anyway, as long as nothing makes them manage it.\nB:  `family cookbook is online`  ·  `recetario familiar está en línea`  --  plain, but every other place here is online too.\nC:  `family cookbook is the spell cloud`  ·  `recetario familiar es la nube de encanto`  --  says whose it is, and needs a brand decision first.\nWhy:  Keep.  Also meant to work with no place line, `- it is saved in the cloud`, which doesn't compile yet.\nRenaming it later:  2 lines in 2 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "family cookbook is the cloud",
      "recommended": "family cookbook is the cloud"
    },
    {
      "id": "place-type",
      "section": "place",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/CreatePlace.ts",
      "line": 29,
      "purpose": "The word for the idea itself, which a person meets when a library adds a kind of place.",
      "note": "Spanish:  `un proyecto supabase es un lugar`\nToday:  Half built:  English compiles and is tested.  Spanish doesn't work:  `lugar` isn't a type there, so the line makes a class of nothing, and the next line, `recetario familiar es un proyecto supabase en \"x\"`, is refused.\nA (built, recommended):  `a supabase project is a place`  --  plain, works for this device and a server alike, and in Spanish.\nB:  `a supabase project is a home`  ·  `un proyecto supabase es un hogar`  --  warm, but reads oddly for a server, and every program would lose the name `home`.\nC:  `a supabase project is a service`  ·  `un proyecto supabase es un servicio`  --  fine for servers, untrue of this device or a file.\nWhy:  Not offered:  `source`, `store`, `record`, `sync`, `backend`, all ruled out in the word list.  Spell only used `place` inside code before.  `lugar` needs adding to Spanish, whichever word stays.\nRenaming it later:  5 lines in 5 files of rules, tests and docs, 1 line in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "a supabase project is a place",
      "recommended": "a supabase project is a place"
    },
    {
      "id": "are-at",
      "section": "details",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 11,
      "purpose": "Where this kind of thing is on that server, when it isn't where you'd expect.",
      "note": "Spanish:  `- las recetas están en \"/platos\"`\nToday:  Compiles;  no test runs it against a server with its own address.\nA (built, recommended):  `- recipes are at \"/dishes\"`  --  the plural of the place line's own `at`.\nB:  `- recipes live at \"/dishes\"`  ·  `- las recetas viven en \"/platos\"`  --  warmer, but `vive en` was why `lives in` lost for the type's own line.\nC:  `- its recipes are at \"/dishes\"`  ·  `- sus recetas están en \"/platos\"`  --  says whose, like the place's other details, but can read as a property being declared.\nWhy:  Reads as what it means.\nRenaming it later:  12 lines in 10 files of rules, tests and docs, 8 lines in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "- recipes are at \"/dishes\"",
      "recommended": "- recipes are at \"/dishes\""
    },
    {
      "id": "is-called",
      "section": "details",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 14,
      "purpose": "The name a field goes by over there, when the server calls it something else.",
      "note": "Spanish:  `- el título de una receta se llama \"nombre_plato\"`\nToday:  Runs:  the recipe box, on real servers.\nA (built, recommended):  `- a recipe's title is called \"dish_name\"`  --  a whole sentence anybody can read.\nB:  `- a recipe's title is \"dish_name\" there`  ·  `- el título de una receta es \"nombre_plato\" allí`  --  short, but it looks like setting the title to that text.\nC:  `- a recipe's title goes by \"dish_name\"`  ·  `- el título de una receta se conoce como \"nombre_plato\"`  --  clear, and more of a mouthful in both languages.\nWhy:  The only place this sentence shape appears, and it reads first time.\nRenaming it later:  39 lines in 31 files of rules, tests and docs, 3 lines in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "- a recipe's title is called \"dish_name\"",
      "recommended": "- a recipe's title is called \"dish_name\""
    },
    {
      "id": "versions-in",
      "section": "details",
      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/places/places.en.ts",
      "line": 29,
      "purpose": "Which field holds a thing's version number, so Spell can tell when someone changed it first.",
      "note": "Spanish:  `- sus versiones están en \"version\"`\nToday:  Runs:  the recipe box, on a real REST server.\nA (built, recommended):  `- its versions are in \"version\"`  --  short, and parallel with `its ids are in`.  `Its` here is the place's, so a thing's plural reads fine.\nB:  `- a recipe's version is in \"version\"`  ·  `- la versión de una receta está en \"version\"`  --  more exact, singular, per type:  longer, and a place usually has one rule for all its types.\nC:  `- it counts versions in \"version\"`  ·  `- cuenta las versiones en \"version\"`  --  says what the number does, but it doesn't have to be a count.\nWhy:  Few people will write it.\nRenaming it later:  5 lines in 5 files of rules, tests and docs, none in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "- its versions are in \"version\"",
      "recommended": "- its versions are in \"version\""
    },
    {
      "id": "ids-in",
      "section": "details",
