      "file": "parser/src/spell/rules/expressions/Or.ts",
      "line": 19,
      "purpose": "Either of two values.  It compiled to something always true until this epic fixed it.",
      "note": "Spanish:  `cada receta donde su cocinero es \"Ana\" o \"Bo\"`\nToday:  Runs, and tested in English;  the Spanish compiles.\nA (built, recommended):  `every recipe where its cook is \"Ana\" or \"Bo\"`  --  how anyone says it.\nB:  `every recipe where its cook is \"Ana\" or its cook is \"Bo\"`  ·  `cada receta donde su cocinero es \"Ana\" o su cocinero es \"Bo\"`  --  what programmers learn to write, and worked before.  Also still works.\nC:  `every recipe where its cook is one of \"Ana\" or \"Bo\"`  ·  `cada receta donde su cocinero es uno de \"Ana\" o \"Bo\"`  --  unambiguous, and already works, but people wouldn't write it first.\nWhy:  Studies of people new to programming find they say `and` for this, so `and` of one thing also warns, offering this form.\nRenaming it later:  6 lines in 3 files of rules, tests and docs, 3 lines in the built-in projects, none in your own projects.",
      "current": "every recipe where its cook is \"Ana\" or \"Bo\"",
